> If all this folding and this monoid nonsense feels sketchy and scary, I suggest watching [Antoine Leblanc’s video essay on Monads](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LekhueQ4zVU) as a good place to begin. Similarly, I suggest reading through their blog https://nauths.fr/en/ as well as Gabby’s blog https://haskellforall.com/ for general discussions involving Haskell and functional programming paradigms.

## data flow, concurrent folds, Monoid stuff 🖖

Alrighty, so at the expense of making this way more complex than any of this needs to be in a quest to really put the **simulation** into 
`sms_simulation.md`, we are making some sizable tradoffs I'll need to defend; in particular, we are giving up roughly 3x CPU time reduction
in a drastically simpler (ie. whatever a data structures & algorithms (DSA) textbook solution would suggest) implementation for certainty and 
replayability.  

Before going further, please bear in mind in this problem, throughput is set by M, P and message time in flight (which in this case, is mostly sleeps; we are mostly waiting around.

### ⊕ Summary is a monoid which each Sender process accumulates folds into it and the Monitor folds the senders' folds

> "Fold" is a standard FP term for reducing a sequence to one value where you combine the elements with a binary operation starting from an initial value.

If the monoid is also commutative- such as our ⊕ Summary, order and grouping don't change the result. That's why any order of senders, and any split of the ids into chunks reach the same total.

- In Haskell, we can do this with foldr/foldl. Over a monoid it's `foldMap f: map each element with f`, then combine the results with Semigroup combine operator <>, starting from mempty
- In Python, it's functools.reduce. sum(xs, start)

Each sender computes one message's fate with deliver(cfg, i) (fate.py:98): a few SplitMix64 draws, microseconds of CPU. 

it then sleeps through that message's latency, about 100 ms by default. We can state the per-message CPU is roughly 1/10,000 of the time in flight. Throughput is about min(λ, P·M/E[τ]), which is set by latency and the rate, not by cores. 

Under the GIL the sleeping senders already overlap, because sleep and Valkey socket I/O release the GIL.  You can find my exploration of freethreading in a later document. 

![The data flow: ovals are processes and rectangles are data](./dfd.svg)

| name | kind | what it does |
|---|---|---|
| Pipeline | process | creates the Queue, starts the Producer, M Senders (or P workers of M) and the Monitor; `scale(m)` while it runs |
| Producer | process | makes ids 0 to N − 1 as Messages, puts each and sleeps until the next is due |
| Message | data | `Message(id, body, created_us)`: body `fate.body(s, id)`, `created_us` its due time |
| Queue | data | at most C messages; `put` blocks at C, `get` hands message to a sender or `None` (stop), `done` commits, `report` passes a sender's Summary |
| Sender | process | `get`, `gateway.send`, `done`, `report` its own Summary; a last report at stop |
| Stats | data | each sender's latest Summary (local), or the totals hash the commits increment (stream) |
| Monitor | process | sums Stats every T and prints a snapshot; at the end the total and `check` |

A sender owns its Summary and posts copies to the Monitor: one dict under the queue's lock (threads), a stats queue
(processes), or the totals hash and the `snap:<name>` keys (stream). 

No sender reads another's total.   All senders share one μ and one p, so a total never depends on which sender took which message.

## Weeding through QuickCheck lore on SplitMix64 implimentations

Find below the confluence of a bunch of reading and exploring and hacking on this via the QuickCheck docs; I used 
my local LLM model and harness to iterate on this in Haskell. 

Every random choice is a pure function of (seed s, message i, attempt j, purpose). Words are 64-bit, mod 2⁶⁴
(`src/adi_sms/fate.py`, restated in `spec/model/Fate.hs`):

```text
# THis is what I ended up with after a fair bit of reading, headscratching and slopping around:

# I stole the algo from here https://prng.di.unimi.it/splitmix64.c, which then points to 
# https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/util/SplittableRandom.html
# I got the constant words from David Stafford's post here, which I mention in my video. 
# http://zimbry.blogspot.com/2011/09/better-bit-mixing-improving-on.html
# Stafford refers to this as his Mix13.

mix(z)        = z ^= z >> 30; z *= 0xBF58476D1CE4E5B9; z ^= z >> 27; z *= 0x94D049BB133111EB; return z ^ (z >> 31)
absorb(h, w)  = mix((h ^ w) + 0x9E3779B97F4A7C15)
key(s, i)     = absorb(absorb(0, s), i)
draw(k, p, j) = absorb(k, (p << 56) | j)             purposes LEN=1 BODY=2 LAT=3 FAIL=4 ARR=5
length(k)     = draw(k, LEN, 0) mod 101
body(s, i)    = the first length(k) little-endian bytes of draw(k, BODY, 0), draw(k, BODY, 1), ..., as ALPHABET[byte & 63]
latency(k, j) = floor(-mu * log1p(-u)),  u = (draw(k, LAT, j) >> 11) * 2^-53
fails(k, j)   = draw(k, FAIL, j) < floor(ppm * 2^64 / 10^6)
gap(s, i, l)  = -log1p(-u) / l seconds,  u = (draw(k, ARR, 0) >> 11) * 2^-53;  0 when l = 0
```

## Summary monoid and exactly-once

A `Summary` is (sent, failed, attempts, time_us) with componentwise addition and identity 0 is literally a commutative monoid. Let
run(a, b) be the sum of deliver(i) over the ids a to b − 1.  This is exciting!  

If each id in 0 to N − 1 is committed exactly once, the committed total equals run(0, N). That holds for any number of
senders, any senders started or stopped, any assignment of ids and any completion order. deliver(i) depends only on
the configuration and i, never on the sender, its level or the time. A finite sum in a commutative monoid does not
depend on order or bracketing. `fold_equivalence` in 03-properties tests it.

`sms fold` is the oracle for every run. An atomic take (local) or the commit script (stream) supplies the exactly-once
premise. `sms fold --threads T` sums T id ranges to the same total. Counts match on every platform, and `time_us`
matches where processes share one libm (`log1p`).

## backpressure and bounded memory

The queue holds at most C messages, and `put` blocks while it is full. Shedding load is not built and AFAICT shouldn't be, 
as this would break our exactly-once total.  in my whiteboard video I falsely state at most once a few times I think, which
doesn't make a whole lot of sense.  I'll probably speed up the WB sessions anyway.

## atomic commit at bigger scales, such as on stream

A pod's sender is one consumer of the group `senders`, named `<pod>-<k>`. Each loop tries
`XAUTOCLAIM sms senders <me> <lease> 0-0 COUNT 1` (an entry held past the lease, 30 s by default), then
`XREADGROUP ... COUNT 1 BLOCK 1000 STREAMS sms >`, then `GET stop` (exit 0 or loop again). After the send it commits
with one script, `src/adi_sms/commit.lua`, run with EVALSHA:

```lua
if redis.call('XACK', KEYS[1], 'senders', ARGV[1]) == 0 then return 0 end -- not new: count nothing
redis.call('HINCRBY', KEYS[2], 'sent', ARGV[2])
-- ... failed, attempts, time_us, and done += 1
redis.call('XDEL', KEYS[1], ARGV[1])
return 1
```

Valkey runs a script *without* interleaving, so an ack, count and the delete either happen together or not at all. 


The count must branch on the ack's result in one server-side step:

| option | why not |
|---|---|
| MULTI/EXEC | cannot branch on XACK's result inside the transaction |
| WATCH, then MULTI | an optimistic retry: under contention the retries grow with M |
| a set of done ids | costs O(N) memory, and N is unbounded |
| a Valkey function | also Lua, plus a library to load for the same seven lines |

**Leases.** Every T, and at least three times per lease, `sms consume` re-claims its senders' messages with
`XCLAIM ... 0 <id> JUSTID`. That resets their idle time, so only a dead or stalled pod's messages are reclaimed.

**Exit codes.** A pod exits 0 once `stop` is "1". On a scale-down's SIGTERM its senders commit the message in hand and
write their snapshots, then it exits 143, which the Job's failure policy ignores. A sender thread that dies of an error
stops the pod with exit 1, which counts toward the Job's `backoffLimit`.

**Halt.** The demo's halt button, or Ctrl-C on `sms monitor`, sets `halt` and `stop` to "1". The producer exits 0 at
its next put. The senders drain the queue and exit 0. A halted run prints its partial totals with `"check": false`.
